%N A400369 Minimum number of vertices of any simple undirected graph with exactly n triangles.
%C A400369 Sequence computed using Brendan McKay's graph data and the Mathematica function GraphTriangleCount.
%C A400369 a(n) is the smallest m such that A263340(m,n) > 0. - _Pontus von Brömssen_, Oct 01 2026
%H A400369 Brendan McKay, <a href="https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/data/graphs.html">Simple graphs</a>
%e A400369 The smallest graph containing exactly one triangle is the complete graph on three vertices, hence a(1) = 3. More generally, if n is a number of the form m*(m-1)*(m-2)/6, then the complete graph on m vertices has n triangles, and a(n) = m.
%e A400369 Among the simple graphs on four vertices, one has four triangles and one has two triangles, so a(2) = a(4) = 4.
%e A400369 The smallest graph with three triangles has five vertices, so a(3) = 5.
%Y A400369 Cf. A263340.
%K A400369 nonn,new
%O A400369 1,1
%A A400369 _Jonas Karlsson_, Sep 24 2026
%E A400369 a(61)-a(92) from _Pontus von Brömssen_, Oct 01 2026
